# The 60 MHz oscillator and the six FPGA LEDs of a Great Scott Gadgets
# Cynthion r1.4.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHERE A8 COMES FROM, AND WHAT IT IS NOT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# 1. **Great Scott Gadgets' own Amaranth platform file**,
#    `cynthion/python/src/gateware/platform/cynthion_r1_4.py` in
#    `greatscottgadgets/cynthion`:
#
#        # Primary, discrete 60MHz oscillator.
#        Resource("clk_60MHz", 0, Pins("A8", dir="i"),
#            Clock(60e6), Attrs(IO_TYPE="LVCMOS33")),
#
#    Not `PinsN`, so the clock is not inverted, and `Clock(60e6)` is the
#    rate this design's blink is derived from.
#
# 2. **The r1.4.0 KiCad schematic** in `greatscottgadgets/cynthion-hardware`,
#    sheet `clock_misc.kicad_sch`: `Y1`, a SiTime
#    `SIT1602BC-23-33E-60.000000E`, 3.3 V, 60.000000 MHz, to A8. A
#    single-ended CMOS oscillator, which is why a single-ended input on
#    this ball is the right thing.
#
# 3. **Project Trellis' `iodb.json`** for the caBGA-256, which puts A8 at
#    row 0, column 29, PIO **B**, bank 0 — the top edge — and gives that
#    PIO the pin function `PCLKC0_0`.
#
# That last line is the one worth reading twice. `PCLKT0_0` and `PCLKC0_0`
# are the true and complement halves of bank 0's differential clock pair,
# both at column 29: PIO A is the T half and PIO B is the C half. Only the
# **T** half has the dedicated path into the clock centre — in `TMID_0`'s
# `bits.db`, `G_JPCLKT00 <- W1_JINCK` and `G_JPCLKT01 <- JINCK`, whose
# `JPADDI` fixed connections name `PIOA` two and four columns west.
#
# So the board's oscillator is on the half **without** the dedicated path,
# and this clock reaches its buffer the way nextpnr routes a clock from any
# other pin: through ordinary interconnect into one of the `PCLKCIB` wires
# that a `DCC`'s input mux offers (`G_JTLQPCLKCIB0 <- S1W6_JD7` and its
# three siblings, also in `TMID_0`). `docs/fpga-trellis.md` has the walk.
#
# Bank 0's VCCIO is 3.3 V, hence LVCMOS33: all three of Great Scott
# Gadgets' own bitstreams set `BANK.VCCIO` to `3V3` on every bank of the
# part, `BANKREF0` included, and the oscillator is a 3.3 V part.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE LEDS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Ball names from the same platform file:
#
#     *LEDResources(pins="E13 C13 B14 A15 D12 C11",
#                   attrs=Attrs(IO_TYPE="LVCMOS33"), invert=True),
#
# in that order, so `led_n[0]` is E13. All six are on the top edge in
# bank 1. `invert=True` is the polarity: they are active low, and a
# constraints file cannot say so. See `clock_blink.v`.

set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 clk A8

set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led_n[0] E13
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led_n[1] C13
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led_n[2] B14
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led_n[3] A15
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led_n[4] D12
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led_n[5] C11
